            "text_G4bG4k": {
              "type": "text",
              "name": "t:names.description",
              "settings": {
                "text": "<p>gnès appeared as if summoned from a Regency reverie, pedaling through the East Sussex countryside with a basket of peonies riding ahead of her. The kind of indulgence one only sees in fairytales. It was there, where hedgerows felt like corridors and gardens behaved like confidants, that I first met her.</p><p>She shares the somewhat indignant tale of her aristocrat-horticulturalist mother, whom she calls \"gardening royalty.\" \"My mother's love was nurtured in the garden, alone. I began painting flowers as a quiet act of inheritance, possibly bestowing on them the devotion I had learned to observe but never received.\"</p><p>We linger over the cliché: English breakfast tea, homemade marmalade, fresh clotted cream from her farm, warm rhubarb-filled scones. She describes art school in Paris, its long halls and stern instruction, where her daydreams were disciplined but never dismissed. A girlfriend who ran away with her favorite leather jacket and her heart. Agnès noticed early how light lingered on petals longer than on people, and came to believe, quite seriously, that beauty was something one could cultivate with patience and hope.</p><p>Her whimsical countryside still carries that early wonder. Part memory, part envy. As if each bloom were painted not just as it was, but as it wished to be remembered.</p><p></p><p>xo Leonie Jannoit</p><p></p>",
                "width": "100%",
                "max_width": "none",
